  "Habitual (invariant) 'be' is one of the best-documented features of African American English: 'My grandmother be cooking every Sunday' means she regularly cooks, an aspect distinction General American English has to express with words like 'usually.' 'She is cooking' means right now, so the teacher's correction actually changes the meaning. Recognizing AAE as rule-governed lets teachers build style shifting toward academic English without treating the home variety as broken.",
  [None,
   T("TERM", "The form follows the rules of a systematic variety of English. It isn't a developmental gap in a second-language learner's system.", "dialect feature ↔ interlanguage"),
   T("DEFICIT", "Calling a rule-governed dialect feature 'slang' that lacks grammar is a deficit judgment. Habitual be follows consistent grammatical rules."),
   T("PARTIAL", "It is an AAE feature, but the meaning differs: 'be cooking' marks a regular, habitual event, while 'is cooking' means right now. The teacher's correction changes the meaning.")],
