Q("1.7-08", "knowledge", 2,
  "A colleague describes Haitian Creole as 'broken French' and suggests that Haitian students' home language is not a real language. Which response is most accurate linguistically?",
  ["Haitian Creole is a pidgin: a simplified contact language with no native speakers, used for limited purposes such as trade.",
   "Haitian Creole is a dialect of French, so students who speak it can easily understand French-language school materials.",
   "Haitian Creole is a simplified form of French, so it offers little foundation for transferring literacy and concepts to English.",
   "Haitian Creole is a creole: a full, rule-governed language with native speakers and its own grammar, drawing much vocabulary from French."],
  3,
  "A pidgin is a simplified contact language with no native speakers; when a pidgin becomes a community's native language, it expands into a creole, a complete language with its own consistent grammar. Haitian Creole (Kreyòl) draws much of its vocabulary from French but has its own grammar (for example, tense and aspect are marked with particles such as 'te' and 'ap' before the verb), and it is an official language of Haiti. Calling it 'broken French' is a judgment about status, not a linguistic fact.",
  [T("TERM", "A pidgin has no native speakers. Haitian Creole is the first language of millions of people and has a complete grammar.", "creole ↔ pidgin"),
   T("TERM", "Haitian Creole has its own grammar and is not fully mutually intelligible with French, so French materials can't be assumed comprehensible to Haitian Creole speakers.", "creole ↔ dialect"),
   T("DEFICIT", "Treating the home language as 'simplified' and useless for transfer is a deficit view. Literacy and concepts developed in Haitian Creole transfer to English like those in any language."),
   None],
  "A creole is a full native language that grew from a pidgin; Haitian Creole has its own grammar and is an official language of Haiti."),

